Team,

Before this becomes another standing report, can we answer the real question?

Collections wants a prioritisation model, but validation must separate defaul=
t risk from the effect of different contact strategies. The possible stories =
are that contact channel changes promise-kept rates, that high-risk customers=
 need tailored arrangements rather than more attempts, or that some customers=
 self-cure without intervention.

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use February 2022 as the new evidence point rath=
er than recycling the earlier conclusion. The operating teams agree that more=
 journeys are failing; they disagree on whether the customer, the bank or a d=
ownstream party owns the failure. There are enough data exceptions to make a =
polished average dangerous; preserve the unresolved cases.

Please prepare a working Excel file with the exception population and clear c=
olumns. The output must help us decide whether a model can support treatment =
allocation and what experiment is needed next.

Data available: collections cases, promises to pay and recovery payments; cus=
tomer contacts, complaints and suggestions; transaction-level timestamps, cha=
nnels, amounts, statuses and error context; the latest closed loan files avai=
lable by the request date, including affordability, pricing and origination r=
isk fields.

Please state the limitation plainly: Observational data cannot prove treatmen=
t causality; recommend a controlled test where appropriate.

Thanks
